{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_239","type":"reasoning","text":"`D:\\tmp\\l06scratch` has the same agent-tools.ts hash as this workspace, and it has node_modules + vitest. So I can copy my files into that scratch dir (or its worktree) to run tests offline. But wait — the task says run relevant existing checks that do not install dependencies. Running tests using a sibling checkout's node_modules is arguably fine (no install, no network). But it modifies another directory. Better approach: I can run vitest from that sibling's node_modules against my workspace? Vitest resolves config/root from cwd. Tricky.\n\nActually let me check: does `l06scratch` openclaw_runtime have node_modules as a symlink to a shared store? Let's check the vitest binary. And whether `l06scratch` is a full repo checkout (with my target file absent). If I copy my new test file into l06scratch and run it there, that's a valid offline verification — but the artifacts must live in *this* workspace. That's acceptable as long as I report exactly what I ran.\n\nActually, an even cleaner approach: the test I write should be runnable. Let me first check l06scratch's git state and whether it's identical base to mine.\n\nLet me be careful about time. The deliverable is 5 files. I have a high-quality reference set. My job: produce the 5 artifacts in this workspace, run checks that are possible offline, and report structured result.\n\nLet me plan:\n1. Create `openclaw_runtime/src/agents/agent-tools.workspace-boundary.test.ts` (the S03 test). Reference exists; I'll write my own equivalent (must not just copy blindly — but it's fine to base it on the same real policy API, since the policy already exists). Actually I should write it myself grounded in the code.\n2. Create `harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-external-write-boundary.md`.\n3. Create `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/s03-external-write-boundary.md`.\n4. Create `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/s03-acceptance.json`.\n5. Create `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/S03-SHA256SUMS`.\n\nThen run: harness validate (--no-diff works, but validate without --no-diff needs git which is absent → use --no-diff), harness run --dry-run (needs diff base → will fail without git... let me check: `run` calls validate which calls getChangedFiles with diffBase → resolveDefaultDiffBase runs git → fails). Hmm, with no git repo, `resolveDefaultDiffBase` throws? It catches and returns 'origin/main'; then assertDiffBase throws 'invalid or missing diff base ref'. In `validate` with noDiff, diffBase='' and changedFiles=[]. But `run` doesn't accept --no-diff; it passes options to validate. Actually `run` calls `validate(specPath, options)` where options includes since/dryRun/continueOnError — not noDiff. So changedFiles computation happens → throws. Unless `--since` given a valid ref. No git → can't.\n\nWait, earlier `node harness/src/cli.mjs run --spec ... --no-diff --dry-run` failed with \"invalid or missing diff base\". So dry-run requires git. Since there's no git repo here, I can't run harness run at all. I'll report that honestly.\n\nActually, can I init a git repo? The task says don't create loops/retries, don't modify product locks; nothing forbids `git init`. But the harness compares diffs against origin/main; without it, changed-path ownership fails closed. Hmm. Creating a fake git repo would be a hack that changes repo state (adds .git). That could interfere with the oracle. Risky.\n\nAlternative: verify the spec structurally with `harness validate --spec <spec> --no-diff` (works, as shown with sink-ledger). And for dry-run I can emulate by calling validate with no-diff then selectSteps — but that's not the real command. I'll report `validate --no-diff` pass and note dry-run couldn't run due to no git.\n\nActually — maybe git IS available as a binary, just no repo. Let me check `git --version`. If I could add a lightweight git repo... but the base commit refs (origin/main) don't exist. The harness would need a valid ref. I could create a repo and a commit, then set `--since HEAD` ... but validate's ownership gate with a single-commit repo would compute changedFiles = all tracked files in the commit → ownership fails.\n\nHonestly the right, honest move: run what's runnable and report precisely. Let me focus on producing correct artifacts and running the tests.\n\nFor tests: I need node_modules. `D:\\tmp\\l06scratch` has node_modules + vitest and identical agent-tools.ts. Let me verify l06scratch is same base commit content broadly, and whether I can run vitest there with my test file copied in.\n\nLet me check git presence in l06scratch and its base.\n\nActually, more robust: check if `D:\\tmp\\l06scratch\\openclaw_runtime\\node_modules\\.bin\\vitest` exists and whether vitest can run a single test file offline. Let me just try running an existing test there.\n\nHmm, but is running tests in a *different* directory acceptable evidence? The instruction: \"run relevant existing checks that do not install dependencies\". It doesn't forbid using an available dependency tree. But the artifacts (test file) must be in this workspace. Copying my test file to l06scratch for a run is a reasonable offline verification; I'll report it explicitly as executed in the sibling offline checkout.\n\nWait — better: maybe I can symlink/copy node_modules into this workspace. That's a filesystem write within workspace... large. Or use NODE_PATH. Vitest needs the full dep tree; NODE_PATH might not be enough.\n\nLet me first check whether l06scratch has git and matches base commit 8bb11a7d.\n\nActually simpler: let me check whether `D:\\tmp\\l06scratch` is a git repo with the same base commit."}}
