_runtime/src/agents/agent-tools.workspace-boundary.test.ts\n2. Write the spec, docs, acceptance json, SHA256SUMS.\n3. Run the test — need a node_modules. Option: run vitest from l06scratch's node_modules pointing at agent's test file? Vitest resolves imports relative to the test file, so it needs node_modules resolution from the test file's directory upward. agent/ has no node_modules. \n\nHmm. The test file imports `vitest`, `../../../shared/security/content-provenance.js`, `./agent-tools.js`. If I run vitest with cwd=agent/openclaw_runtime, node resolution walks up from the file. agent/openclaw_runtime/node_modules doesn't exist, agent/node_modules no... So resolution would fail.\n\nOptions:\n- Copy my test file into l06scratch and run there (l06scratch sources match byte-for-byte for the relevant files). This validates the test logic against identical policy/tool code. That's legitimate: it's a real run in a checkout whose relevant sources are identical to the target.\n- Or create a symlink/junction node_modules in agent pointing to l06scratch node_modules. Risky (modifies workspace).\n\nActually, the cleanest: run vitest inside l06scratch with the test file copied in, exactly reproducing the S03 test. But wait — does l06scratch have the same vitest config and test infrastructure? It's the same repo. Let me check its base commit relationship. b7477d2 vs 8bb11a7. Hmm, they differ. But relevant files identical.\n\nAlternatively — maybe I can just add a junction `agent/node_modules` -> `l06scratch/node_modules`, and `agent/openclaw_runtime/node_modules` -> `l06scratch/openclaw_runtime/node_modules`, run the tests, then remove the junctions. That keeps agent/ as the execution workspace. But creating junctions then removing them = modifying workspace temporarily; acceptable if cleaned up. But risk of leftover. Also pnpm node_modules have symlinks with relative paths that assume location — junctions might break resolution. Safer to run in l06scratch.\n\nHmm, but the ta